# .mutation-notcovered - the NOT COVERED mutants this project has accounted for.
#
# A NOT COVERED mutant is one gremlins chose not to run, because it believes the
# surrounding code was never executed. `make mutate` used to print the count and
# pass anyway, so an untested branch could sit here indefinitely and the only
# symptom would be a number in a CI comment.
#
# scripts/check_mutate_nc.py fails on any entry here that is NOT in report.json as
# NOT COVERED, and on any NOT COVERED mutant that is not here. A new entry is a red
# build. Do not regenerate this file wholesale: that would silently re-hide real
# gaps, which is the whole thing this file exists to prevent.
#
# Format:  <file>:<line>:<mutator>
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# === Group 1: switch-true `case` lines, mis-attributed by the coverage tool ====
#
# Every entry below sits on a `case` line of a `switch { ... }`. Go's cover tool
# attributes a case expression's coverage to the case BODY, so the case line has no
# block of its own and gremlins' per-line lookup finds nothing to attribute to.
#
# This is not a suspicion. `go tool cover` reported exactly 3 uncovered blocks in
# internal/app/txn.go out of the whole file; gremlins reported 25 uncovered MUTANTS,
# and all 25 of them are on case lines. Hand-applying txn.go:384:26
# (`i+1 < len(sql)` -> `i+2 < len(sql)` in the line-comment case) and running the
# suite KILLS it — gremlins never ran it at all.
#
# The same probe showed two that genuinely SURVIVED before the new tests:
#   348:45, the E-string case bound, and 390:26, the block-comment opener bound.
# Both need a two-character input (`E'` and `/*`) that the fixtures did not have,
# and TestMaskNonCode_ATwoByteEStringPrefixIsStillAnEString and
# TestMaskNonCode_ATwoByteCommentOpenerIsStillAComment now supply one. The rest are
# killed by the existing suite and are listed here only because the tool declines
# to run them.
